# #105 r5 CORRECTED expectation (was a must-NOT-be-a-must-accept anti-loop
# probe under r4b, reclassified as a genuine POSITIVE control under r5):
# self-referential var field (RecSh{val, next: var RecSh}) reached through
# THREE DIFFERENT import aliases (p1/p2/p3) on the two recursion sides.
# Each alias's own registered ObjectDef copy self-references ITS OWN
# alias-qualified name (e.g. p1's copy's `next` field resolves back to
# p1's own copy), so oa/ob are never pointer-identical across the p1-vs-p2
# comparison -- resolving the recursive `next` field lands back on the
# exact same (oa, ob) pair every level, a genuine period-1 cycle over a
# SINGLE PHYSICAL declaration (RecSh) reached through 3 aliases: this is
# semantically a positive control (same physical type, must compile), not
# a "does the anti-loop budget still fire" probe.
#
# r4b's depth<=0 -> die() was the ONLY termination path available and
# fired on this legal case: review A (fnsig105_review_a_r4b_verdict.md
# §2.3) independently proved with an isolated mutual-cycle construction
# that r4b's depth budget cannot distinguish "genuinely cyclic and equal"
# from "genuinely cyclic and different" -- both burn the same budget and
# both die(), a false rejection for every legal recursive/mutually-
# recursive var-field type reached through import aliases. r4b's own
# comment on this fixture ("Expected: clean rc=2 ... proves anti-loop not
# broken") was exactly the misdiagnosis review A called out: it observed
# rc=2 and read it as "the backstop engaged correctly" rather than "the
# backstop is the ONLY termination path and it necessarily fires here".
#
# Under r5's coinductive visited-pair fix, comparing (RecSh@p1, RecSh@p2)
# pushes that pair as "in progress", then recurses into (RecSh@p1.next's
# resolved target, RecSh@p2.next's resolved target) which resolves back to
# the SAME (RecSh@p1, RecSh@p2) pair -- now found already in progress, so
# the coinductive shortcut returns true immediately, one level in, never
# touching the depth budget. Expected on `fixed` (r5): rc=0, exec=18
# (o.val=42 -> handler returns o.val+1000=1042, truncated to an 8-bit
# process exit code: 1042 mod 256 = 18).
import tests/cheng/backend/fixtures/fnsig105_r4b_selfref_impl
import tests/cheng/backend/fixtures/fnsig105_r4b_selfref_reg
import tests/cheng/backend/fixtures/fnsig105_r4b_selfref_base as p3

fn main(): int32 =
    let rc: int32 = fnsig105_r4b_selfref_impl.fnsig105R4bSrRegister()
    var o: p3.RecSh
    o.val = 42
    return fnsig105_r4b_selfref_reg.fnsig105R4bSrInvoke(o)
